You're trying to make check_version() return true. (This is a necessary
but not sufficient condition; check_version() can be fooled, but it's not
a good idea.)

check_version compares four things from the version_info struct,
created by make_version() in util/makedefs.c:

* incarnation

  If you change the version numbers (VERSION_MAJOR, VERSION_MINOR,
  PATCHLEVEL, and EDITLEVEL) in include/patchlevel.h, then you'll
  have to make sure that VERSION_COMPATIBILITY in the same file is
  set for the oldest version numbering that you want to claim
  compatibility with.

* feature_set

  This is mostly for checking differences between compilations, not
  patches; if you don't add anything to the list of features that
  gets written into this, you'll be ok. (On the other hand, if you
  do add a #define that breaks compatibility, you _should_ add it
  here.)

* entity_count

  This is where the objects and monsters come in; the total numbers of
  artifacts, of different objects, and of monster types has to be
  identical. (If you rearrange them without altering their number, you
  can sneak it past this check, but then you'll get mysteriously
  transmutating stuff when loading old bones or saves.)

* struct_sizes

  The structs flag (all sorts of miscellaneous stuff; you can avoid this
  check by putting your flag in the instance_flags struct instead, but
  that means it won't persist across sessions), obj (object properties),
  monst (individual monster properties, as opposed to those pertaining
  to all monsters of a given type), and you (things that need to be
  remembered about the character) have to be the same size. Adding a
  new option that changes the flag struct is a common way to break
  compatibility here.


> (For instance, I'm guessing that perhaps a cause of the most recent
> bones incompatibility is that I renamed one of my new monsters -
